    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Having a small garden (a small yard, in US terms) does not mean you have to give up on a proper outdoor living space. In fact, compact gardens often have a more intimate, "secret garden" feel that larger plots lack. The key to a successful small deck is clever geometry, multi-functional features and making use of height. By thinking carefully about how you use every bit of space, you can turn a cramped patch of grass into an inviting timber retreat that feels larger than it is.</p>

<h2 id="multi-level">1. Multi-level</h2>
<p>One of the most effective ways to make a small garden feel bigger is to build on more than one level. Instead of one flat surface, a two-tiered deck creates distinct "rooms" for different activities. You might use the upper level for a small bistro table and the lower level for a cosy reading nook. The change in level breaks up the view and gives the space more depth and interest. Keep the steps between levels generous and even, and remember that higher platforms may need guarding and approval under local building regulations.</p>

<h2 id="integrated-benches">2. Integrated benches</h2>
<p>In a small space, bulky garden furniture quickly becomes an obstacle course. Built-in seating is one of the best space-savers. By building benches into the deck's edge or railing, you avoid extra chairs that take up floor space. Benches can also act as a visual edge to the deck, and with lift-up lids they provide useful storage for garden tools or outdoor cushions. On a raised deck, check whether your local regulations still require a proper guard, as a bench alone may not count.</p>
<!--figure:balcony-lounge-string-lights|A covered lounge with timber bench seating, cushions, wall planters and string lights at dusk: built-in seating like this frees up floor space on a small deck-->

<h2 id="diagonal-planking">3. Diagonal planking</h2>
<p>The way you lay your boards can change how big the deck feels. Boards laid parallel or at right angles to the house are standard, but laying them diagonally, at 45 degrees, creates a sense of movement. It draws the eye towards the far corners, making the area appear wider and more considered. It is a simple design tweak that gives a custom look. Bear in mind that diagonal boards usually need closer joist spacing, often 300 mm (12 in) centres, and create more cutting waste, so check the board manufacturer's guidance before you start.</p>

<h2 id="corner-nooks">4. Corner nooks</h2>
<p>If your garden is particularly tight, do not try to centre the deck in the middle of the lawn. Instead, tuck a floating deck into a corner. Corner decks use space that often goes to waste and create a sheltered, private feel. This layout leaves the rest of the garden open for planting or a small lawn, so the deck does not dominate the whole space. It turns a dead zone into a highlight of the garden.</p>
<!--figure:garden-lawn-planting|A garden lawn bordered by hydrangeas and ornamental grasses: keeping the deck to one corner leaves room for planting and grass like this-->

<h2 id="vertical-privacy">5. Vertical privacy</h2>
<p>When you cannot expand outwards, expand upwards. Small gardens are often overlooked by neighbours, so privacy is usually a priority. Instead of a standard railing, consider a tall, slatted timber privacy screen. It adds an architectural element to the deck and gives you a backdrop for vertical planting. You can hang planters, festoon lights or even a projector screen for outdoor film nights. Check local height limits for screens and fences near boundaries, as taller structures may need permission.</p>

<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Before you build, mark the deck out with string and set your chairs and table inside it. In a small garden, a deck that is slightly too small for the furniture is a common frustration, and this test shows it straight away.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
<h3>What is the best wood for a small deck?</h3>
<p>For small decks, dense hardwoods such as ipe or cumaru are worth considering because you need less material, which makes their higher price easier to absorb. Cedar and redwood are also popular for their natural rot resistance and warm colour. Pressure-treated softwood is the usual budget choice. Look for FSC-certified timber where you can.</p>
<h3>How can I make my small deck look bigger?</h3>
<p>Use lighter stains to reflect light, choose slim-profile railings such as cable or glass, and keep the styling simple. Avoid oversized furniture and choose pieces with slender legs that show more of the deck floor.</p>
<h3>Do I need a permit for a small deck?</h3>
<p>It depends on where you live and the deck's height. In England, decking is generally permitted development if it is no more than 30 cm (12 in) above the ground and, with outbuildings, covers no more than half the garden. Many US codes exempt freestanding decks under 76 cm (30 in) that are not attached to the house. Flats, listed buildings and conservation areas have stricter rules, so always check with your local planning or building authority first.</p>
<h3>Can I put a fire pit on a small wood deck?</h3>
<p>Only with care, and only if your fire pit and decking manufacturers allow it. Follow the fire pit manufacturer's clearances, place it on a heat-resistant mat or pad, keep it well away from railings, screens and overhanging plants, and never leave it unattended. A gas fire table is often considered better suited to timber decks than a wood-burning pit, but check both manufacturers' guidance and any local rules first.</p>
<h3>How much does a small wood deck cost?</h3>
<p>It depends mainly on the size, the timber you choose, how high the deck sits and whether you build it yourself. Pressure-treated softwood is usually the cheapest option, while hardwoods cost noticeably more. Extras such as built-in seating, multiple levels, railings and steps all add to the price. To compare quotes fairly, ask for itemised quotes to the same specification, covering frame, boards, fixings, railings and waste removal. Read our <a href="/blog/wood-deck-cost">wood deck cost guide</a> for more detail.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>A small garden can carry a beautifully planned deck as long as every element works hard: built-in seating, clever board layouts and screening that adds privacy without closing the space in. For the practical side, read <a href="/blog/how-to-build-a-floating-deck">how to build a floating deck</a> and <a href="/blog/wood-deck-pros-and-cons">wood deck pros and cons</a>, or get styling ideas from <a href="/blog/small-balcony-decorating-ideas">small balcony decorating ideas</a>. Explore our <a href="/services/balcony-terrace-decoration">balcony and terrace decoration</a> service, or <a href="/book-consultation">book a consultation</a> to plan your garden with a designer.</p>
